# trash.imap, with the connection dropped instead of a LOGOUT answer
# Account.MoveToTrash: find the \Trash folder, select the folder, read the
# messages' headers for the log, move them. Hand-written (see login_list.imap)
S: * OK [CAPABILITY IMAP4rev1 AUTH=PLAIN] IMAP4 service ready
C: a1 LOGIN "someone@example.com" "app-pass"
S: a1 OK [CAPABILITY IMAP4rev1 UIDPLUS MOVE] user logged in
C: a2 LIST "" "*"
S: * LIST (\HasNoChildren \Trash) "/" "Deleted Messages"
S: * LIST (\HasNoChildren) "/" "INBOX"
S: a2 OK LIST completed
C: a3 SELECT "INBOX"
S: * 3 EXISTS
S: a3 OK [READ-WRITE] SELECT completed
C: a4 UID FETCH 7:8 (UID FLAGS RFC822.SIZE INTERNALDATE BODY.PEEK[HEADER.FIELDS (DATE FROM TO CC SUBJECT MESSAGE-ID IN-REPLY-TO REFERENCES REPLY-TO)])
S: * 1 FETCH (UID 7 FLAGS () RFC822.SIZE 100 INTERNALDATE " 3-Nov-2026 09:15:00 -0600" BODY[HEADER.FIELDS (DATE FROM TO CC SUBJECT MESSAGE-ID IN-REPLY-TO REFERENCES REPLY-TO)] {#}
L: From: Store <deals@example.net>
L: Subject: Sale
L: Message-ID: <sale@example.net>
L:
S: )
S: a4 OK FETCH completed
C: a5 UID MOVE 7 "Deleted Messages"
S: * 1 EXPUNGE
S: a5 OK MOVE completed
C: a6 LOGOUT
X: the connection drops
